RG.deep["across-all-worlds"] = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Word study: ‘held together’, ‘summed up’, ‘reconciled’",
      body: [
        "Three Greek verbs carry the weight of this theme. In Colossians 1:17 Paul says that in Christ all things <em>synestēken</em>. The verb means to stand together, to cohere. Greek writers used it for the way a thing is composed and holds as one. It is in the perfect tense: all things have come to stand together in him and go on doing so.",
        "In Ephesians 1:10 God’s plan is <em>anakephalaiōsasthai ta panta en tō Christō</em>, ‘to sum up all things in Christ’. The root is linked to <em>kephalē</em>, ‘head’, and to <em>kephalaion</em>, a ‘sum’ or main point. Paul uses the same verb in Romans 13:9, where all the commandments are ‘summed up’ in love of neighbour. Christ is to creation what love is to the Law: the point where everything comes together and makes sense.",
        "In Colossians 1:20 God reconciles all things through Christ. The verb is <em>apokatallassō</em>, a strengthened form of the usual word for reconciliation. Lexicons note that it is not found in earlier Greek literature or the Greek Old Testament; Paul may have coined it. Some scholars hear in the prefix <em>apo-</em> the idea of bringing back to a former harmony."
      ],
      refs: ["Col 1:17", "Eph 1:10", "Rom 13:9", "Col 1:20", "Eph 2:16", "Col 1:21–22"]
    },
    {
      title: "Close reading: Colossians 1:15–20 in its setting",
      body: [
        "The poem has two matching halves. Verses 15–17 say Christ is ‘the firstborn of all creation’ and that ‘all things have been created through him, and for him’. Verses 18–20 say he is ‘the firstborn from the dead’ and that through him God reconciles ‘all things’. The repeated words, firstborn and all things, tie the halves together. The one who made everything is the one who heals everything.",
        "Notice the list in verse 16: ‘thrones or dominions or principalities or powers’. The Colossians seem to have feared spiritual powers (2:8, 18). Paul’s answer is not that the powers are unimportant, but that they were made through Christ, for Christ, and are disarmed in him (2:15). Nothing in the universe stands outside his reach.",
        "Then Paul turns to the readers: ‘You, being in past times alienated… yet now he has reconciled’ (1:21–22). The cosmic and the personal belong together. The same reconciliation that reaches ‘things in the heavens’ reaches a frightened believer in a small town in Asia Minor. Verse 23 adds ‘if you continue in the faith’, which reminds us that this truth is meant to be lived in, not just admired."
      ],
      refs: ["Col 1:15–20", "Col 1:16", "Col 2:8", "Col 2:15", "Col 1:21–23", "John 1:3", "Heb 1:2–3"]
    },
    {
      title: "The wider biblical pattern",
      body: [
        "Colossians is not alone. John says ‘all things were made through him’ (John 1:3). Hebrews says the Son upholds ‘all things by the word of his power’ (Hebrews 1:3). Paul tells the Corinthians there is ‘one Lord, Jesus Christ, through whom are all things, and we live through him’ (1 Corinthians 8:6). In Acts 17:28 he quotes a Greek poet to pagans: in God ‘we live, and move, and have our being’.",
        "The Old Testament prepares for this. Wisdom is pictured beside God at creation, ‘rejoicing in his whole world’ (Proverbs 8:30–31). The psalmist cannot escape God’s presence even in Sheol (Psalm 139:7–10). The New Testament writers saw in Christ the Wisdom and Word through whom God made and keeps the world."
      ],
      refs: ["John 1:3", "Heb 1:3", "1 Cor 8:6", "Acts 17:28", "Prov 8:22–31", "Ps 139:7–10"]
    },
    {
      title: "How the early church read it",
      body: [
        "Irenaeus (c. 180) built his whole picture of salvation on Ephesians 1:10. In his Latin translators’ word, Christ <em>recapitulated</em> humanity: he went back over Adam’s story and ran it again rightly, from birth to death, so that what was lost in Adam might be recovered in him.",
        "Athanasius (c. 318) made a bold point about the incarnation. Even while the Word lived in one human body in Galilee, he did not stop holding the universe together. He was ‘actually in everything’, yet distinct from creation and never absorbed into it. That is the ancient answer to the fear of pantheism: God is present in all, but is not the same as all.",
        "Maximus the Confessor (seventh century) later spoke of the one Logos and the many <em>logoi</em>, the inner ‘words’ or meanings of created things. He taught that every creature has its true meaning in Christ."
      ],
      refs: ["Eph 1:10", "Rom 5:12–19", "1 Cor 15:45", "John 1:14"]
    },
    {
      title: "The strongest counter-reading, and a response",
      body: [
        "Many careful scholars argue that ‘reconcile all things’ in Colossians 1:20 includes the subduing of hostile powers, not their friendship with God. Colossians 2:15 speaks of the powers being disarmed and triumphed over. On this reading, ‘reconciliation’ for rebels means being put back in their place, a kind of imposed peace.",
        "That reading deserves respect. A response is that Paul immediately applies the very same verb to the Colossians themselves, who were once ‘enemies in your mind’ and are now reconciled to be presented ‘holy and without defect’ (1:21–22). In Ephesians 2:16 the same verb describes Jew and Gentile made one and brought to God. Elsewhere in Paul the word always means restored relationship. Whether it can mean less here is a fair question; the text does not settle every question about final outcomes, but it points in a hopeful direction."
      ],
      refs: ["Col 1:20", "Col 2:15", "Col 1:21–22", "Eph 2:14–16", "Phil 2:10–11", "Isa 45:22–23"]
    },
    {
      title: "Practical meaning",
      body: [
        "If all things hold together in Christ, then no part of your life is outside him: not your body, your work, the natural world, or your darkest memories. He is already there. Prayer becomes less about bringing God into a place and more about noticing that he is in it.",
        "It also shapes how we treat creation and other people. The forest, the river and the stranger all exist through him and for him. Care for the earth and kindness to people are not side issues. They are ways of honouring the One in whom everything stands."
      ],
      refs: ["Col 3:11", "Eph 4:6", "Rom 8:19–21", "Ps 24:1"]
    }
  ],
  evidence: [
    { text: "Thayer’s lexicon gives the sense of <em>synistēmi</em> in Colossians 1:17 as “to cohere, hold together: τά πάντα συνέστηκεν ἐν αὐτῷ”.", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, Strong’s G4921 (Bible Hub)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/4921.htm" },
    { text: "HELPS Word-studies: <em>anakephalaiomai</em> “shows the head as the ‘organizing center,’ causing all the parts to work together in harmony.” It occurs only in Romans 13:9 and Ephesians 1:10.", source: "HELPS Word-studies, Strong’s G346 (Bible Hub)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/346.htm" },
    { text: "Thayer defines <em>apokatallassō</em> as “to reconcile completely” (others, “to reconcile back again, bring back to a former state of harmony”) and notes it is “Found neither in secular authors nor in the Greek O. T.”", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, Strong’s G604 (Bible Hub)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/604.htm" },
    { text: "“He was not only in it, but was actually in everything, and while external to the universe, abode in His Father only.”", source: "Athanasius, On the Incarnation of the Word, 17 (NPNF translation)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2802.htm" },
    { text: "“When He became incarnate, and was made man, He commenced afresh the long line of human beings… so that what we had lost in Adam — namely, to be according to the image and likeness of God — that we might recover in Christ Jesus.”", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.18.1", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103318.htm" }
  ]
};

