RG.deep["inclusion"] = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Word study: ‘in him’ and ‘all died’",
      body: [
        "The little phrase ‘in Christ’ or ‘in him’ (<em>en Christō</em>, <em>en autō</em>) runs all through Paul’s letters. In Ephesians 1:3–14 alone it appears again and again: blessed in him, chosen in him, redeemed in him, sealed in him. For Paul, salvation is not first a transaction but a location. It is about where we are: in the Son.",
        "In 2 Corinthians 5:14 Paul reasons: ‘one died for all, therefore all died’. The word <em>hyper</em> (‘for’) means on behalf of. The logic only works if Christ is a representative head. When the head acts, the whole body is involved. Verse 15 then adds the goal: ‘that those who live should no longer live to themselves’."
      ],
      refs: ["Eph 1:3–14", "2 Cor 5:14–15", "Rom 6:3–6", "Gal 2:20"]
    },
    {
      title: "Close reading: the two Adams",
      body: [
        "Romans 5:12–21 is the backbone of inclusion theology. Paul sets two figures side by side: Adam and Christ. ‘As through one trespass, all men were condemned; even so through one act of righteousness, all men were justified to life’ (5:18). The ‘all’ on each side has the same grammar.",
        "1 Corinthians 15:22 runs the same way: ‘as in Adam all die, so also in Christ all will be made alive’. Paul did not think we chose to be ‘in Adam’. We were born into a humanity already shaped by him. Inclusion teachers argue that, in the same way, Christ has taken hold of humanity before we choose him.",
        "Romans 5:17 adds an important note: it speaks of ‘those who receive the abundance of grace’. Paul holds together the universal scope of Christ’s act and the personal receiving of it. A careful theology of inclusion has to keep both."
      ],
      refs: ["Rom 5:12–21", "Rom 5:17", "Rom 5:18", "1 Cor 15:22", "1 Cor 15:45–49"]
    },
    {
      title: "How the early church read it",
      body: [
        "Athanasius explained the cross in exactly these terms. The Word took a body like ours and gave it to death ‘in the stead of all’, so that all being counted as dead in him, the law of ruin might be undone. He speaks of humanity as a whole, not only of believers.",
        "Gregory of Nazianzus, defending the full humanity of Jesus, gave the church a rule of thumb: what the Son did not take on, he did not heal. The flip side is the good news of inclusion: what he did take, our whole human nature, he healed. Irenaeus said the Son of God became Son of Man so that humanity, taken into the Word and receiving adoption, might become children of God.",
        "These fathers also taught baptism, faith and judgment. They are not modern inclusionists. But the conviction that Christ’s humanity is the place where all humanity is met and healed is deeply theirs."
      ],
      refs: ["Heb 2:14–17", "John 1:14", "Col 2:9–10", "Gal 4:4–5"]
    },
    {
      title: "Barth and Torrance: included, but not by a system",
      body: [
        "Karl Barth reframed election. Instead of a hidden decree sorting people, he said election is first God’s choice in Jesus Christ, who is both the God who chooses and the human who is chosen. Barth would not say all will certainly be saved, nor that some certainly will be lost. He thought that question belonged to God’s freedom.",
        "T. F. Torrance, writing in 1949, warmly admired the hope behind universalism, saying there was ‘a great truth here’, yet he rejected universalism as a logical system. He held that Christ died for all and that humanity is bound to him, while also holding that a person can still refuse the love that holds them. That tension is typical of serious inclusion theology.",
        "This is very different from the later teaching of Carlton Pearson, whose ‘gospel of inclusion’ moved toward a broad pluralism and denial of hell, and which fellow Pentecostal bishops declared heretical in 2004."
      ],
      refs: ["Eph 1:4", "1 Tim 2:5–6", "1 Tim 4:10", "John 12:32"]
    },
    {
      title: "The strongest counter-reading, and a response",
      body: [
        "The most serious objection is that Paul’s ‘in Christ’ is always tied to faith and baptism. ‘You are all children of God, through faith in Christ Jesus. For as many of you as were baptized into Christ have put on Christ’ (Galatians 3:26–27). Romans 8:9 says that if anyone does not have the Spirit of Christ, ‘he is not his’. On this view, the ‘all’ of Romans 5 means all who are in Christ, as the ‘all’ of Adam means all who are in Adam.",
        "A response is to distinguish two levels that Paul seems to hold together: what Christ has done for and with humanity (objective), and our Spirit-given sharing in it (subjective). Faith and baptism are how we come to live in what is true. They do not create the truth. The objection is still weighty, and each side should admit that Paul’s language can be read in both directions."
      ],
      refs: ["Gal 3:26–27", "Rom 8:9", "John 3:18–19", "Rom 5:17", "2 Cor 5:20"]
    },
    {
      title: "Practical meaning",
      body: [
        "Inclusion changes the tone of evangelism. Paul writes ‘be reconciled to God’ right after saying God ‘was in Christ reconciling the world to himself’ (2 Corinthians 5:19–20). The appeal is urgent, but it is an invitation to come home, not a sales pitch.",
        "It also changes how we see ourselves. Your belonging does not rise and fall with your spiritual performance. It rests on what the Son has done with your humanity. That is the ground on which real repentance and change can grow."
      ],
      refs: ["2 Cor 5:19–20", "Luke 15:20–24", "1 John 4:19", "Rom 2:4"]
    }
  ],
  evidence: [
    { text: "“He gave it over to death in the stead of all… to the end that, firstly, all being held to have died in Him, the law involving the ruin of men might be undone.”", source: "Athanasius, On the Incarnation of the Word, 8", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2802.htm" },
    { text: "“For that which He has not assumed He has not healed; but that which is united to His Godhead is also saved.”", source: "Gregory of Nazianzus, Letter 101 (To Cledonius)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3103a.htm" },
    { text: "“The Word of God was made man, and He who was the Son of God became the Son of man, that man, having been taken into the Word, and receiving the adoption, might become the son of God.”", source: "Irenaeus, Against Heresies III.19.1", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/0103319.htm" },
    { text: "Torrance wrote that the church “has consistently judged universalism as a heresy for faith and a menace to the Gospel”, yet “there is a great truth here and that must be disentangled from the dangerous untruth.”", source: "T. F. Torrance, “Universalism or Election,” Scottish Journal of Theology 2 (1949), abstract", url: "https://tftorrance.org/1949-033" },
    { text: "“In 2004, the Joint College of African-American Pentecostal Bishops Congress declared Pearson’s teaching about hell to be heretical.”", source: "Associated Press obituary of Carlton Pearson (via TheGrio, 2023)", url: "https://thegrio.com/2023/11/21/carlton-pearson-influential-oklahoma-megachurch-founder-who-rejected-hell-dies-at-age-70/" }
  ]
};

