RG.deep["apokatastasis"] = {
  sections: [
    {
      title: "Word study: one word, one verse",
      body: [
        "The noun <em>apokatastasis</em> appears only once in the New Testament, in Acts 3:21. Peter says heaven must receive Jesus ‘until the times of restoration of all things’. Outside the Bible the word was used for restoring a building, a person’s health, or an exile to their home. Astronomers used it for stars returning to their starting point.",
        "The related verb, <em>apokathistēmi</em>, appears in Acts 1:6, where the disciples ask, ‘Lord, are you now restoring the kingdom to Israel?’ So in Acts the idea grows from Israel’s hope to the renewal of ‘all things’ promised by ‘all his holy prophets’.",
        "An honest note: the Greek of Acts 3:21 can be read as ‘the restoration of all the things that God spoke through the prophets’. On that reading, the phrase means the fulfilment of everything promised, not necessarily the salvation of every person. Scholars differ."
      ],
      refs: ["Acts 3:19–21", "Acts 1:6", "Matt 17:11", "Isa 65:17", "Rev 21:5"]
    },
    {
      title: "Close reading: 1 Corinthians 15:20–28",
      body: [
        "This passage is the main text for many universal-hope writers. Paul lays out an order: Christ the firstfruits, then those who belong to Christ at his coming, then ‘the end’, when he hands the kingdom to the Father after abolishing every hostile power (15:23–24). Death itself is ‘the last enemy’ to be done away with (15:26).",
        "The goal is ‘that God may be all in all’ (15:28). Gregory of Nyssa read this as meaning that evil will have no place left anywhere, because God will fill everything. Others read ‘all in all’ as God’s total rule, which can include judged enemies. Both readings take the verse seriously.",
        "Romans 11:32 and Philippians 2:10–11 are often read alongside. In Philippians, ‘confess’ is <em>exomologeō</em>, the same verb Jesus uses when he says ‘I thank you, Father’ (Matthew 11:25). Universalists argue that this is joyful praise, not forced surrender. Traditional readers point to Isaiah 45:24, where enemies are ‘put to shame’."
      ],
      refs: ["1 Cor 15:20–28", "Rom 11:32", "Phil 2:10–11", "Matt 11:25", "Isa 45:22–24"]
    },
    {
      title: "Punishment that corrects?",
      body: [
        "Matthew 25:46 uses <em>kolasis</em> for punishment. Aristotle drew a famous distinction between <em>kolasis</em>, punishment for the good of the one who suffers, and <em>timōria</em>, punishment that satisfies the one who inflicts it. Universalists often cite this to argue that Jesus chose a corrective word.",
        "Fairness requires the other half. Lexicons note that later Greek did not always keep Aristotle’s distinction, and Jewish and early Christian texts used <em>kolasis</em> for the penalties of the next world. The word allows a corrective sense but does not prove it.",
        "George MacDonald pressed the corrective reading from the character of God rather than from grammar. God’s fire, he preached, does not burn us because we refuse him; it burns until we are free to love him."
      ],
      refs: ["Matt 25:46", "1 John 4:18", "Heb 12:5–11", "Mal 3:2–3", "1 Cor 3:13–15"]
    },
    {
      title: "How the early church read it",
      body: [
        "Origen held that ‘the end is always like the beginning’: God’s goodness will draw all things back to one end through Christ and the Spirit. He also taught that souls existed before birth, an idea the church later rejected.",
        "Gregory of Nyssa taught that God’s judgment is not mainly punishment but a painful separating of good from evil, so that the soul can enter communion with God. He read ‘God all in all’ as ‘the complete annihilation of evil’.",
        "Augustine, in the West, argued firmly the other way. If ‘eternal’ punishment ended, he said, ‘eternal’ life would have to end too, since Jesus used the same word for both. His view shaped Western teaching for over a thousand years.",
        "The first of the fifteen anathemas linked to 553 condemns anyone who teaches ‘the fabulous pre-existence of souls’ and ‘the monstrous restoration which follows from it’. Notice the wording: it ties the condemned restoration to pre-existence. Whether it condemns every form of hope for all is still debated."
      ],
      refs: ["1 Cor 15:28", "Matt 25:46", "Acts 3:21"]
    },
    {
      title: "The strongest counter-reading, and a response",
      body: [
        "The strongest case against universal salvation is the cumulative weight of Jesus’ warnings: the narrow gate (Matthew 7:13–14), the unforgivable sin ‘neither in this age, nor in that which is to come’ (Matthew 12:32), the great gulf (Luke 16:26), and Revelation’s ‘forever and ever’ (Revelation 14:11; 20:10). Add to this the long agreement of most of the church.",
        "Hopeful universalists answer that these warnings are real and describe a terrible possibility, while the purpose texts (1 Timothy 2:4; Romans 11:32; Colossians 1:20) describe what God intends and will not give up on. Revelation itself pictures the city’s gates never shut (21:25) and leaves ‘for the healing of the nations’ (22:2). The debate is between two sets of texts. Humility on both sides is wise."
      ],
      refs: ["Matt 7:13–14", "Matt 12:32", "Luke 16:26", "Rev 14:11", "Rev 21:25", "Rev 22:2", "1 Tim 2:4"]
    },
    {
      title: "Practical meaning",
      body: [
        "Whatever you conclude, this hope changes prayer. Paul urges prayers ‘for all men’ because God ‘desires all people to be saved’ (1 Timothy 2:1–4). You can pray for the worst person you know with real hope.",
        "It also changes how we speak of judgment. Judgment is not God’s love switching off. It is love refusing to leave evil in place. That makes judgment serious and fearsome, and also good news."
      ],
      refs: ["1 Tim 2:1–4", "Lam 3:31–33", "Ezek 18:23", "1 Cor 13:8"]
    }
  ],
  evidence: [
    { text: "Thayer: <em>apokatastasis</em> means “restoration: τῶν πάντων, the restoration not only of the true theocracy but also of that more perfect state of (even physical) things which existed before the fall, Acts 3:21.” It occurs once in the New Testament.", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, Strong’s G605 (Bible Hub)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/605.htm" },
    { text: "“God will be all in all… and herein it appears to me that Scripture teaches the complete annihilation of evil.”", source: "Gregory of Nyssa, On the Soul and the Resurrection", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2915.htm" },
    { text: "“It is not punishment chiefly and principally that the Deity, as Judge, afflicts sinners with; but He operates… only to get the good separated from the evil and to attract it into the communion of blessedness.”", source: "Gregory of Nyssa, On the Soul and the Resurrection", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/2915.htm" },
    { text: "“For the end is always like the beginning… the goodness of God, as is worthy of Him, incites and attracts all to that blissful end, where all pain, and sadness, and sorrow fall away and disappear.”", source: "Origen, De Principiis I.6.2", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/04121.htm" },
    { text: "“If both destinies are eternal, then we must either understand both as long-continued but at last terminating, or both as endless.”", source: "Augustine, City of God XXI.23", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/120121.htm" },
    { text: "“If anyone asserts the fabulous pre-existence of souls, and shall assert the monstrous restoration which follows from it: let him be anathema.”", source: "Anathemas against Origen, 1 (linked to the Second Council of Constantinople, 553)", url: "https://www.newadvent.org/fathers/3812.htm" },
    { text: "Thayer notes Aristotle’s distinction of <em>kolasis</em> (disciplinary, with reference to the one who suffers) from <em>timōria</em> (penal, for the satisfaction of the one who inflicts), but adds: “usage (especially the later) does not always recognize the distinction.”", source: "Thayer’s Greek Lexicon, Strong’s G2851 (Bible Hub)", url: "https://biblehub.com/greek/2851.htm" },
    { text: "“It is not that the fire will burn us if we do not worship thus; but that the fire will burn us until we worship thus.”", source: "George MacDonald, “The Consuming Fire”, Unspoken Sermons, Series I", url: "https://gutenberg.org/cache/epub/9057/pg9057-images.html" }
  ]
};

